WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.903
Last time we saw that an array

2
00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:06.790
has a fixed capacity.

3
00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:09.910
Here is one with room for four items

4
00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:11.900
and it is full.

5
00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:14.400
Now a fifth item arrives.

6
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.485
We can't put it just after the block

7
00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:17.837
because that memory may belong

8
00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:19.050
to something else.

9
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.634
And yet the lists in your phone and your

10
00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:24.390
browser never say the list is full.

11
00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:25.800
So what do they do?

12
00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:28.693
The answer is a dynamic array

13
00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:30.373
and a surprisingly beautiful

14
00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:31.580
piece of counting.

15
00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:33.856
We'll see how to grow an array

16
00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:35.480
why the size of the growth

17
00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:37.280
matters enormously

18
00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:40.010
and what amortized cost means.

19
00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:42.120
Here is the trick.

20
00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:45.280
When the array is full, we reserve a new

21
00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.070
bigger block somewhere else.

22
00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:49.796
Then we copy every item from the

23
00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:52.700
old block into the new one, in order.

24
00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:55.160
The old block is no longer needed

25
00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:56.880
so we let it go.

26
00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.570
And now there is room for the new item.

27
00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:01.651
Notice what that cost

28
00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:04.006
one copy for each item that

29
00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:05.040
was already there.

30
00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:07.910
How much bigger should the new block be?

31
00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:11.420
The simplest idea: just one slot bigger.

32
00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:13.474
Then the next append finds

33
00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:15.280
the array full again

34
00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:17.500
and we copy everything again.

35
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:18.820
And again.

36
00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:22.410
Every single append copies the whole array.

37
00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:26.950
Appending n items copies one, plus two

38
00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:30.170
plus three, up to n minus one items.

39
00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:33.550
That adds up to n times n minus one

40
00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:34.760
over two.

41
00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:38.590
For ten items, forty-five copies.

42
00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:39.930
For a thousand

43
00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:42.070
almost five hundred thousand.

44
00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:44.410
For four thousand items

45
00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:46.760
nearly eight million copies.

46
00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.270
Now try the other idea

47
00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:50.610
when the array is full

48
00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:53.140
make the new block twice as big.

49
00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:57.890
Capacity one, two, four, eight, sixteen.

50
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.028
Most appends find free space

51
00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:01.920
and cost nothing extra.

52
00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:05.050
Only when the array fills up do we copy.

53
00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:10.410
And the copies are one, two, four, eight

54
00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:13.640
each time as many as the old capacity.

55
00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:17.620
Add them up: for ten items, fifteen copies.

56
00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:20.560
A hundred items, one hundred twenty-seven.

57
00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:23.680
A thousand, one thousand twenty-three.

58
00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:26.910
Four thousand, four thousand ninety-five.

59
00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:29.982
Compare four thousand ninety-five copies

60
00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:32.014
with nearly eight million.

61
00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:34.064
The copying total is less than

62
00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:35.820
twice the number of items.

63
00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:37.450
Here's why.

64
00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:38.990
Each time we double

65
00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:44.480
from capacity c to two c, we copy c items.

66
00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:46.841
But the array was half empty right

67
00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:48.677
after the previous doubling

68
00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:50.794
so those c items include c over

69
00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:52.540
two that arrived since.

70
00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:55.310
Only the newest half did any new work.

71
00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:58.010
Picture it as paying with coins.

72
00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:00.310
Every append pays three coins

73
00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:03.860
one to write itself, and two to put away.

74
00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:05.822
By the time we need to copy

75
00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:08.155
each of the newest c over two items

76
00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:09.860
has put away two coins.

77
00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:12.160
That is c coins

78
00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:15.370
exactly enough to copy all c items.

79
00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:17.766
So three coins per append always

80
00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:19.030
covers the copying.

81
00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:21.480
The cost per append is constant.

82
00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:25.229
This is called amortized constant time

83
00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:27.301
the cost averaged over a long

84
00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:29.100
sequence of operations.

85
00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:32.607
It does not say that every append is cheap.

86
00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:34.757
The append that triggers a resize

87
00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:36.580
copies the whole array

88
00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:39.490
that single append is big-O of n.

89
00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:42.974
What it promises is that in any long run

90
00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:46.302
the total is at most a small constant

91
00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:48.476
times the number of appends.

92
00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:51.224
Rare expensive appends are paid

93
00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:53.080
for by many cheap ones.

94
00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:55.670
Doubling has a price.

95
00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:57.330
Right after a resize

96
00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:58.990
half the array is empty

97
00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:01.420
so up to half the memory is spare.

98
00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:04.180
Real libraries pick a growth factor that

99
00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:07.300
balances speed against wasted space.

100
00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:09.920
Some double, some grow by half

101
00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:11.038
some by an eighth.

102
00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.616
Any fixed factor greater than one keeps

103
00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.570
the cost per append constant.

104
00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:17.957
What does not work is adding

105
00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:19.590
a fixed number of slots.

106
00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:21.790
That is the one-slot idea again

107
00:04:21.790 --> 00:04:23.560
and it is quadratic.

108
00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:25.170
To sum up.

109
00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:26.930
When an array is full

110
00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:29.970
a dynamic array allocates a bigger block

111
00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:33.460
copies the items and drops the old block.

112
00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:36.880
Growing by a constant factor, like doubling

113
00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:39.591
makes appends amortized constant time.

114
00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.443
Growing by a constant number of

115
00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:43.850
slots makes them quadratic.

116
00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:45.110
Next

117
00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:47.430
a completely different way to store a list

118
00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:48.710
no block at all

119
00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:51.000
just items that point to each other.

120
00:04:51.626 --> 00:04:54.216
Let's write a dynamic array in Python

121
00:04:54.216 --> 00:04:56.386
and count its copies.

122
00:04:56.966 --> 00:04:59.636
The class keeps four things: the block

123
00:04:59.636 --> 00:05:01.736
which is a list of fixed length

124
00:05:01.736 --> 00:05:04.336
starting with just one slot, the size

125
00:05:04.336 --> 00:05:06.176
how many slots are used

126
00:05:06.176 --> 00:05:08.736
a function that says how to grow

127
00:05:08.736 --> 00:05:10.656
and a counter of copies

128
00:05:10.656 --> 00:05:13.206
which we will use to check our arithmetic.

129
00:05:13.556 --> 00:05:15.063
Notice that we never use

130
00:05:15.126 --> 00:05:16.943
the list's own growth.

131
00:05:17.026 --> 00:05:18.891
We make a list of exactly

132
00:05:18.966 --> 00:05:20.126
the length we want

133
00:05:20.126 --> 00:05:22.276
and treat it as a fixed block.

134
00:05:22.856 --> 00:05:25.866
Append first asks: is the block full?

135
00:05:25.866 --> 00:05:28.456
That is when size equals the block's length.

136
00:05:28.806 --> 00:05:31.060
If so, it asks the grow function

137
00:05:31.116 --> 00:05:33.936
for the new capacity, and resizes.

138
00:05:33.936 --> 00:05:36.463
Either way, it then writes the value into

139
00:05:36.516 --> 00:05:37.776
the next free slot

140
00:05:37.776 --> 00:05:39.726
and adds one to the size.

141
00:05:40.306 --> 00:05:42.434
Resize creates a brand new

142
00:05:42.516 --> 00:05:45.546
list of the new length, full of None.

143
00:05:45.896 --> 00:05:49.046
Then a loop copies the items one by one

144
00:05:49.046 --> 00:05:50.696
and counts each copy.

145
00:05:51.046 --> 00:05:54.316
Last, the new list replaces the old one.

146
00:05:54.316 --> 00:05:56.496
Nothing refers to the old list any more

147
00:05:56.496 --> 00:05:59.526
so Python reclaims its memory by itself.

148
00:06:00.106 --> 00:06:02.036
Growth rules are just functions.

149
00:06:02.036 --> 00:06:04.416
Double returns twice the capacity.

150
00:06:04.416 --> 00:06:07.426
Plus-one returns the capacity plus one.

151
00:06:07.776 --> 00:06:10.126
Same class, two behaviours.

152
00:06:10.126 --> 00:06:11.576
Let's watch what happens.

153
00:06:12.156 --> 00:06:15.270
With doubling, we append seventeen items

154
00:06:15.346 --> 00:06:16.972
and print the capacity and the

155
00:06:17.026 --> 00:06:19.036
number of copies after each.

156
00:06:19.386 --> 00:06:24.016
Capacity one, two, four, four

157
00:06:24.016 --> 00:06:26.036
then eight for four appends

158
00:06:26.036 --> 00:06:27.896
then sixteen for eight appends

159
00:06:27.956 --> 00:06:29.909
and at the seventeenth append

160
00:06:29.976 --> 00:06:31.803
it doubles to thirty-two.

161
00:06:31.876 --> 00:06:33.986
The copy count after seventeen

162
00:06:34.056 --> 00:06:38.096
appends is thirty-one: one, two, four

163
00:06:38.096 --> 00:06:39.746
eight and sixteen.

164
00:06:40.326 --> 00:06:43.396
Now we count copies for the two rules

165
00:06:43.396 --> 00:06:45.126
for four sizes.

166
00:06:45.476 --> 00:06:46.914
At a thousand items

167
00:06:46.986 --> 00:06:48.180
doubling made one thousand

168
00:06:48.226 --> 00:06:49.255
twenty-three copies

169
00:06:49.306 --> 00:06:52.354
and plus-one made four hundred ninety-nine

170
00:06:52.426 --> 00:06:54.366
thousand five hundred.

171
00:06:54.366 --> 00:06:56.104
At four thousand

172
00:06:56.206 --> 00:06:57.973
four thousand ninety-five against

173
00:06:58.026 --> 00:07:00.026
almost eight million.

174
00:07:00.026 --> 00:07:01.996
Exactly what the formulas said.

175
00:07:02.576 --> 00:07:04.470
Python's built-in list is

176
00:07:04.546 --> 00:07:06.079
itself a dynamic array.

177
00:07:06.146 --> 00:07:08.472
We can watch its capacity grow by measuring

178
00:07:08.526 --> 00:07:11.016
how much memory the list uses.

179
00:07:11.366 --> 00:07:13.236
On this version of CPython

180
00:07:13.236 --> 00:07:16.516
the capacity jumps from four to eight

181
00:07:16.516 --> 00:07:19.156
to sixteen, twenty-four, thirty-two

182
00:07:19.156 --> 00:07:19.761
and so on.

183
00:07:19.816 --> 00:07:22.221
For large lists it grows by only

184
00:07:22.296 --> 00:07:24.086
about an eighth each time.

185
00:07:24.436 --> 00:07:26.786
That is a smaller factor than two

186
00:07:26.786 --> 00:07:28.816
but still a constant factor

187
00:07:28.886 --> 00:07:31.131
so appends are still amortized

188
00:07:31.206 --> 00:07:32.276
constant time.

189
00:07:32.856 --> 00:07:36.087
So in Python, we got a dynamic array from

190
00:07:36.166 --> 00:07:37.466
a fixed-length list

191
00:07:37.466 --> 00:07:40.506
plus a growth rule and a loop of copies.

192
00:07:40.506 --> 00:07:42.896
Next time: linked lists.

